faq:
  - q: "What is Netflix's hiring process?"
    a: "Netflix's process for most corporate and technical roles: (1) Recruiter reach-out or application. (2) Recruiter screen — culture fit and background. (3) Hiring manager screen. (4) Panel loop — 4–6 rounds with cross-functional interviewers. (5) Offer decision by hiring manager (no committee). Netflix's culture deck is explicit that they hire adults who need no hand-holding — interviewers are assessing culture fit as heavily as technical skill. The process is notably focused on judgment, context-setting, and impact — not process adherence."
  - q: "What is Netflix's culture and how does it affect hiring?"
    a: "Netflix operates on a 'freedom and responsibility' model described in its famous culture deck. The key tenets: (1) No process bureaucracy — employees are expected to make good decisions without approval chains. (2) High performance density — Netflix actively manages out lower performers to maintain a high-caliber team. (3) Transparent information sharing — context and strategy are shared broadly so employees can act with full information. (4) Adults over children — Netflix treats employees like adults and expects self-management. Candidates who thrive here are highly autonomous, excellent communicators, and high performers who do not need structure to execute."
  - q: "How much does Netflix pay compared to other tech companies?"
    a: "Netflix is consistently one of the highest-paying tech companies in the world — often the highest for senior software engineers. Netflix pays primarily in cash (not equity), with salaries sometimes 30–50% above other FAANG companies at equivalent levels. The tradeoff: Netflix's 'keeper test' (would your manager fight to keep you if you resigned?) means lower performers are managed out quickly. High performers are compensated exceptionally; mediocre performers do not last."
  - q: "Does Netflix use coding interviews for software engineers?"
    a: "Yes, but Netflix's coding interviews vary by team and are generally less LeetCode-focused than Google or Meta. Netflix SWE interviews tend to include more system design and architecture than algorithmic puzzle-solving — particularly for senior and staff levels. The cultural interview component is significant: Netflix interviewers spend meaningful time assessing judgment, communication, and culture fit. A strong coder with poor judgment and communication will not pass Netflix's bar."
  - q: "What is the 'keeper test' at Netflix?"
    a: "The keeper test is Netflix's management philosophy: 'If someone on my team told me they were leaving for a similar job at another company, would I fight hard to keep them?' If the answer is no, Netflix believes the right thing to do is to let that person go now (with generous severance) and find someone the team would fight hard to keep. This means Netflix's bar is not just 'can this person do the job' — it is 'is this person one of the best people I could find for this job?'"
