      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">Preparing for Netflix interviews</h2>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Read the Netflix culture deck before any interview.</strong> The culture deck is public and specific — it describes exactly what Netflix values and what it does not. Interviewers reference it explicitly. Understanding it is not optional preparation.</li>
        <li><strong>Demonstrate judgment, not just execution.</strong> Netflix interviewers probe decision-making under ambiguity: "What would you do if you had no budget and a hard deadline?" "How did you handle a situation where leadership gave you incorrect context?" Prepare stories that show you made smart decisions with limited information — not just that you executed assigned tasks.</li>
        <li><strong>Be direct and candid.</strong> Netflix values candor — and tests it. If you disagree with something in an interview, say so (respectfully but directly). Generic agreement is a red flag at Netflix. Demonstrate that you have opinions and the communication skill to share them.</li>
        <li><strong>For SWE: system design over algorithms.</strong> Netflix's engineering culture is architecture-heavy. Prepare for system design questions at scale: distributed streaming, content delivery, recommendation systems, and resilience engineering. Coding ability is assumed — judgment and design are what differentiate candidates at senior levels.</li>
        <li><strong>Show impact, not activity.</strong> Netflix's culture deck explicitly separates hard work from results. Every story you tell in a behavioral interview should end with a specific, measurable business impact — not an output (shipped the feature) but an outcome (retention increased by 8%).</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="content-panel">
      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">Netflix compensation strategy</h2>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Netflix pays primarily in cash.</strong> Unlike FAANG peers who offer substantial RSU equity, Netflix's compensation model is heavily weighted toward base salary — often 30–50% above market. This simplifies compensation conversations but requires understanding your true market rate to negotiate effectively.</li>
        <li><strong>Research Netflix salaries on Levels.fyi and Blind.</strong> Netflix has less public data than Google or Amazon — Blind's anonymous community often has the most current Netflix comp data, particularly for senior and staff levels.</li>
        <li><strong>The Netflix negotiation dynamic is different.</strong> Because compensation is cash-heavy, the negotiation is simpler — but Netflix has a reputation for paying at or above market from the initial offer. The negotiation is rarely dramatic. Anchoring to the highest credible market data for your level is the most effective approach.</li>
        <li><strong>Is Netflix right for you?</strong> High performers who want maximum autonomy, top-of-market pay, and no bureaucracy thrive at Netflix. Professionals who need structure, mentorship, or a slower pace may find the culture uncomfortable. Netflix culture fit is a real variable — do your research before targeting Netflix specifically.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
